messages:
maya: Heads up — spotted a weird telemetry/logging interaction in the Node sampleapp during onboarding. When the X-Request-Id header is present, some requests log with tiny float timestamps and level flips to debug randomly. Repro steps below. Anyone seen this?

kevin: hmm, that smells like a boot-time race or monkeypatch ordering. Can you paste the logger + otel init bits?

maya: Sure. Boot order: load config -> init logger -> init OTEL auto-instrumentation -> attach express middleware. Code snippets:```js
// logger.js
const pino = require('pino');
module.exports = pino({ level: process.env.LOG_LEVEL || 'info', timestamp: pino.stdTimeFunctions.isoTime });

// app.js (simplified)
const logger = require('./logger');
require('./telemetry')(); // opentelemetry auto-instrumentation init
app.use((req,res,next)=>{ req.log = logger.child({ reqId: req.headers['x-request-id'] }); next(); });
```

kevin: thanks. Can you share a raw log example showing the float ts + level flip?

maya: Yep. Example lines captured during an ab run:```
maya-service GET /api/foo 200 - 0.000234ms debug { reqId: 'abcd-1234' }
maya-service GET /api/foo 200 - 1617973.1234 info { reqId: 'efgh-5678' }
```
The weirdness: sometimes the timestamp is a tiny fraction (0.000234) and level becomes debug; other times it's a huge float (1.6e6) and level is info. No pattern by endpoint.

sam: do you use any pretty-printing, pino.destination, or pino-multi-stream? Those can swap serializers. Also check any code that patches console methods — otel sometimes wraps console for traces.

maya: We're not explicitly touching console. Removing OTEL init makes the problem disappear. Ordering tests (logger before otel / otel before logger) didn't change it. However, removing the logger.child assignment also makes it go away.

alex: interesting. So child logger + otel auto-instrumentation together trigger it. Child loggers inherit serializers and bindings lazily; if otel monkeypatches a global runtime thing at instrumentation attach time it could alter the timestamp function or level mapping for that child instance.

kevin: can you reproduce with a minimal standalone script (no express) that creates a parent logger, spawns a child per fake-request id, and then triggers http instrumentation? If so, bisect which instrumentation (http/express/core) does it.

maya: I reduced it to this repro:```js
const pino = require('pino');
const { registerInstrumentations } = require('@opentelemetry/instrumentation');
const logger = pino({ level: 'info', timestamp: pino.stdTimeFunctions.isoTime });
const child = (id)=> logger.child({ reqId: id });
// registerInstrumentations(...) // attach http instrumentation
for (let i=0;i<100;i++){ const l = child('id-'+i); l.info('hello'); }
```
With OTEL instrumentation registered, about 20% of lines show the tiny float ts and level debug. Without OTEL, all lines are uniform.

sam: thanks for the minimization. Two likely causes: 1) otel instrumentation alters global Date.prototype.toISOString or replaces time helpers (rare but possible via shims), 2) otel patches stream/console methods and pino child inherits an already-patched binding sometimes and not others due to lazy init.

alex: try forcing pino to use a deterministic timestamp fn on child creation: `const l = logger.child({ reqId: id }); l[pino.symbols.time] = pino.stdTimeFunctions.isoTime` — not public API though. Alternatively, create a wrapper that always calls parent as `logger.bind({ reqId: id })` if using pino v8+, or call `pino({ ...parent.opts })` to create a new independent logger per-request.

kevin: for short-term workaround, set LOG_LEVEL=info globally and avoid child creation in middleware (attach a function that calls logger.info(..., { reqId })) instead of child. Also try upgrading/downgrading the otel instrumentation packages; OpenTelemetry has had a couple of issues with monkeypatch order in v1.20.

maya: tried forcing a no-op timestamp on child by wrapping `logger.child = (o)=> { const c = pino({ level: logger.levelVal, timestamp: pino.stdTimeFunctions.isoTime }); return c.child(o); }` — hacky but reduced frequency.

sam: please file an issue with minimal repro and paste results. Tag infra/eng-ml if it reproduces on their infra. Include node version and otel versions.

ci-bot: Created issue INF-2026 in backlog: "pino child logger + opentelemetry instrumentation causes inconsistent timestamp/level" — link: https://tracker/redwood/INF-2026

maya: Filed PR with minimal repro + notes: https://github.com/redwood-inference/sampleapp/pull/88 — PTAL. If anyone has bandwidth, let's decide on recommended pattern for sampleapp: (A) avoid child loggers in middleware, (B) create independent per-request logger instances, or (C) pin otel version until upstream fix.

alex: I vote A + doc update explaining the race and suggested logging recipe for DX. Also add an onboarding checklist item to run `npm run smoke` which uses the repro harness.

kevin: agree. I'll help add a short section in the devex docs and link INF-2026. :thumbsup:

maya: thanks all — will update PR with doc and temporary workaround.
